Honestly, the scary pictures would wash straight over me. A bowel tumour on a Doritos packet tells me someone wants me to feel guilty, and I've heard enough of that from my sister's Facebook shares to tune it out. The plain text warnings are the same story. Those are the campaigns that assume I've got time to worry about my bowels while I'm trying to get to work.

A calorie number is different. I'm already counting in my head, because I cut the biscuits when my trousers got tight, and a number on the packet is quick to read at the shelf. It also doesn't tell me what to feel. But it's still not the thing that decides it for me. Price per kilo and what's reduced by four o'clock will matter more. So the calorie badge gets most of the weight, and the warnings barely move me.

